Date:     Thu, 1 Apr 93 7:46:03 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Ethnicity, State Schools, History, etc.

I quite disagree with Dale that "ethnicity" is an unextropian concept.  It's
the term for a certain kind of tribalism, and I don't know why tribalism
is necessarily bad.  It can be, but isn't always.

As a spontaneous order, it's a way that people can personally relate to
others that they aren't related to in other ways, such as family.  We
can relate to each other because we speak (or at least read, or get a
translation from) English.  We can do this in spite of the fact that we
have different parents, geographical locations and origins, etc.

I have a theory (also developed under the influence of Leon Llouw) that the
real culprit in all the atrocities that have been blamed on "racism," or
some similar sort of tribalism, is Statism.  The restrained form of my
claim would be that Statism is the worst "ism," worse than racism, sexism,
anti-Semitism, etc.  The unconstrained form of it would be that the State
is the source of all evil (the State itself being rooted in volitional
ignorance: "I don't know and I don't want to know and I won't know!").

As for the question about the ethical curricula of State schools: this
is pretty much SOP.  I just read "Is Public Education Necessary?" by Samuel
Blumenfeld, which is a revisionist history of State schools in the USA
from the first ones in the Massachusetts colony through Horace Mann.  Very
interesting dirt on these institutions, such as:

Under the influence of Robert Owen, who blamed the failure of New Harmony on
the fact that its occupants hadn't been "deprogrammed" from the habits they'd
acquired from early 19-century U.S. society, the upper-class took it upon
themselves to use State schools (first financed by compulsion, with
mandatory attendance laws to follow afterwards) as a means to their end-state
of a socialist utopia.  And all this prior to the coinage of the very term
"socialism."

Their movement entailed the destruction of the many private academies and
charity schools for all but the upper-class.

They explicitly based their ideal schools upon the Prussian model, and
argued for mandatory attendance laws on the explicit analogy to the Prussian
military conscription.

They made control of curricula one of their first goals, sneaking in their
doctrines of Unitarian Christianity under the guise of neutrality between
religious sects.  The practical effect was that Unitarian values were taught,
and orthodox Calvinist ones weren't (and still are, to a large degree).

A fascinating book, and a good example of what benefits history has to offer:
expansion of our knowledge which bears insights upon contemporary affairs.
I've been reading a lot of history recently - three of Paul Johnson's books:
"Modern Times," "Intellectuals," and "Birth of the Modern," all of which I
learned very much from enjoyably (with some small reservations); the above-
mentioned book on public education; "The Counter-Revolution of Science," by
Friedrich Hayek, which the latter half of is a history of the origins and
influence of Positivism from Saint-Simon through Auguste Comte and their
influence upon Hegel and Marx; and now I'm reading "Generations: the History
of America's Future."

Why is it that so few of us seem to be very interested in history?  I've
really enjoyed Perry's argument with Garrett about Jewish history.  I don't
know it very well yet.  I don't think that it's possible to come to very
good predictions of the future without knowledge of the past.  We seem
premature futurists in this way.

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now! - Think Universally, Act Selfishly
starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu

"True greatness consists in the use of a powerful understanding to enlighten
oneself and others." - Francois Marie Arouet Voltaire

