Date:     Thu, 1 Apr 93 8:11:32 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Traffic Improvements, Jews, and Humanism

'Tis odd seeing complex methods of improving traffic flow, when I've been
thinking up simple ways to accomplish the same task recently.  Last night,
for example, I realized that it would be a big help if streetsigns were neon
so that they could be read more easily, especially at night.  Perhaps they
could be made larger, too.  I have a terrible time reading them, and often
have to slow down to do so.  This negatively impacts traffic.

My thinking's something along the lines of: "Simple things private owners
could do to save the roads."

I've gotten the impression that a greater fraction of libertarians fall into
certain demographic categories than in similar camps.  One of the things I've
noticed is that there seems to be a higher fraction of libertarians that are
gay than, say, Repugnants or Demobrats.  Another impression I've gotten is
that a higher fraction of us are Jewish than the Republican party.

Roy Childs' interview in the latest Liberty magazine offers an interesting
explanation: conservatives are anti-semitic, or at least were until recently.
I got the impression from the interview that one of the reasons why Rand
was never accepted by conservatives was her Jewish origin.  Since her
books have made more libertarians out of people than any others (according
to the Liberty poll a few years back - Murray Rothbard, another Jew was
in second place), this would seem to explain something, but I'm not sure
what.  (I am RATHER tired, and will probably read this when I've rested
and cringe at what I wrote!)  Enough of that topic.

(BTW, I heartily reccomend setting aside some time for egregious, gratuitous,
excessive, humorous use of politically incorrect terms for individuals'
race, ethnicity, religion, etc.  Just make it clear to any who might be
offended what you're doing, and hopefully do it in private.  I've always
enjoyed describing my race as "honky.")

Let's see, what else... Ah, yes, humanism.  Craig Presson seems to think it
out of bounds.  But how can we discuss what humans are likely to transcend
into if we don't know as much as possible about what they are already?  I
see transhumanism as the extension of humanism into the future, and often
miss the seeming lack of humanistic background of some posts here.  I, for
one, support all humanistic inquiry which could possibly bear upon our
interest-niche.  (Of course, I tend to exclude space aliens from this
without verifiable evidence...)

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now! - Think Universally, Act Selfishly
starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu

