Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 09:21:56 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: History

Tim Starr asks:

>Why is it that so few of us seem to be very interested in history? . . .
>I don't think that it's possible to come to very
>good predictions of the future without knowledge of the past.  

I agree that it is vital to learn a good deal of history if we want to be
prepared for the future.  History is our best source of empirical
information about how intelligent agents respond to change.

I reccommend Daniel Boorstin's work.  His THE DISCOVERERS is a thrilling
account of how scientific and technological innovations have shaped human
culture.  I understand that THE CREATORS gives a similar treatment to the
arts.  I just finished reading Boorstin's THE AMERICANS: THE COLONIAL
EXPERIENCE, and am now midway through THE AMERICANS: THE NATIONAL
EXPERIENCE.  

Both of these latter two books have led me to appreciate how Extropianism
builds on trends prevalent in American culture.  I've especially enjoyed
comparing the attitudes and experiences of those who explored and settled
the West with those of us who are now probing new frontiers.

