Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 12:14:47 EST
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: BOOKS: re-History

>
> Tim Starr asks:
>
> >Why is it that so few of us seem to be very interested in history? . . .
> >I don't think that it's possible to come to very
> >good predictions of the future without knowledge of the past.

Tom Morrow replies:

> I agree that it is vital to learn a good deal of history if we want to be
> prepared for the future.  History is our best source of empirical
> information about how intelligent agents respond to change.
>
> I reccommend Daniel Boorstin's work.  His THE DISCOVERERS is a thrilling
> account of how scientific and technological innovations have shaped human
> culture.  I understand that THE CREATORS gives a similar treatment to the
> arts.  I just finished reading Boorstin's THE AMERICANS: THE COLONIAL
> EXPERIENCE, and am now midway through THE AMERICANS: THE NATIONAL
> EXPERIENCE.

I wholeheartedly second a recommendation of _The Discoverers_. Its is
probably the best general history of science and discovery. I would
also reccommend avoiding _The Creators_.

Both subjects (science and art histories) are things I have a fair amount
of experience with. I found _The Creators_ to be very lacking in the
strengths of _The Discoverers_. I think it derives from Boorstin's attempt
to overlay the history of creation with the same type of linear development
that he used for science. This fails very miserably and neglects huge
sections of artistic and creative development. It also fails in putting
creative development in a social context. Economic and political influences
besides the obvious are neglected in Boorstin's work. For a general,
knowledgable reader, the book is a nice source of interesting anecdotes
and history about *some* aspects of art history. Boorstin's style is,
as always, totally enjoyable, but unfortunately his expository strengths
are misplaced in _The Creators_.


>
> Both of these latter two books have led me to appreciate how Extropianism
> builds on trends prevalent in American culture.  I've especially enjoyed
> comparing the attitudes and experiences of those who explored and settled
> the West with those of us who are now probing new frontiers.
>
> T.O. Morrow                        --  twb3@midway.uchicago.edu
> Law & Politics Editor: EXTROPY     --  Journal of Transhumanist Thought
> Associate Executive Director: ExI  --  The Extropy Institute

I agree completely on Boorstin's books about America. No other writer
is as capable in capturing the American experience. He is also a
fascinating, if a bit non-lucid, speaker.

Andrew Hall

