Date: Thu,  1 Apr 93 09:28:36 PST
From: hkhenson@cup.portal.com
Subject: State Evil, Schools/Unitarians and History

Tim Starr (in a many-topics post) writes: 

>I have a theory (also developed under the influence of Leon Llouw) that the
>real culprit in all the atrocities that have been blamed on "racism," or
>some similar sort of tribalism, is Statism.  The restrained form of my
>claim would be that Statism is the worst "ism," worse than racism, sexism,
>anti-Semitism, etc.  The unconstrained form of it would be that the State
>is the source of all evil (the State itself being rooted in volitional
>ignorance: "I don't know and I don't want to know and I won't know!").

Erk!  I am no fan of the State, but you would have to stretch 
reasoning beyond the breaking point to make the State the source of 
*all* evil.  The genetic roll of the dice gives us a percent or so of 
folks per generation who cannot be domesticated.  Now, when one of 
those folks get in control of a state . . . .  But this does not make 
the entire concept of a state evil any more than any other 
multipurpose tool is evil

[on State schools--ethical curricula]

>They made control of curricula one of their first goals, sneaking in their
>doctrines of Unitarian Christianity under the guise of neutrality between
>religious sects.  The practical effect was that Unitarian values were taught,
>and orthodox Calvinist ones weren't (and still are, to a large degree).

Once when I was young and foolish, and just bitten with the cryonics 
bug, er, meme, I decided the Unitarians would be a good place to 
spread the cryonics meme.  Boy was *I* wrong!  Perhaps I should post 
to the essay list an article I wrote on my experiences with 
Unitarians.  (They are mostly into liberal guilt.)  But the very 
concept of "Unitarian Christianity" fails to make much sense.  I don't 
believe Unitarians believe in any of the core memes defining 
Christianity.  The Unitarian "religion" seems to be for those who have 
lost all supernatural beliefs but want to maintain the form/social 
contacts that religion provides.  Near as I can tell, most of the 
children raised as Unitarians grow up to be plain atheists.  (Though I 
understand there is an east to west gradient with the east coast 
Unitarians believing in something like god one day in three, and the 
west coast ones doing so one day a month.  :-)  ) 

>Why is it that so few of us seem to be very interested in history?

Most of the extropians I know seem rather knowledgeable about the sweep 
of history.  Some of us have gone very deeply into certain areas, for 
example, about ten years ago I read just about everything available on 
the Black Death.

Re the comments people have been making on the traffic guide project,
I will try to comment on them this weekend.  

Keith

